# A type property has ONE spelling: after a colon.
#
# `type Name pub { ... }` (no colon) used to be accepted and silently discarded.
# That tolerance was added because the parser segfaulted on it, and people write
# it by analogy with `func bar() pub` — but the colon slot is the real mechanism
# (it carries `c_struct`, which the C backend reads), and parse_declaration
# already tells users the canonical spelling is `type Name: pub`. Two spellings
# for one marker, only one of which reaches the AST, is worse than an error.
#
# Both lines below must be rejected; the recorded output is the diagnostics.
# Note the marker does nothing either way — types are always cross-file visible
# in Rae, so `pub` on a type is noise even when spelled correctly. That is why
# the diagnostic says so, and why the codebase does not use it.
import core

type BarePub pub {
  x: Int
}

type BarePriv priv {
  y: Int
}

func main() {
  log("unreachable: this file must not compile")
}
